ow algorithmic management may influence existing power and social structures within organizations. We identify three key issues. First, we explore how algorithmic management shapes pre-existing power dynamics between workers and managers. Second, we discuss how algorithmic management demands new roles and competencies while also fostering oppositional attitudes toward algorithms. Third, we explain how algorithmic management impacts knowledge and information exchange within an organization, unpacking the concept of opacity on both a technical and organizational level. We conclude by situating this piece in broader discussions on the future of work, accountability, and identifying future research steps.\",\"doi\":\"10.1177/20539517211020332\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1177/20539517211020332\",\"sourceId\":\"https://openalex.org/W3178993634\",\"citationCount\":467,\"verificationState\":\"unverified\",\"evidenceId\":\"ev-3937621f-3037-4f43-af68-b6afc40b7b48\",\"projectId\":\"rp-248016a9-4b36-483c-be56-9054c9d4e9ac\"},{\"id\":\"lit-e774f712-4a9c-4a7a-b69f-4fe3be58e143\",\"title\":\"Algorithms as work designers: How algorithmic management influences the design of jobs\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Xavier Parent‐Rocheleau\"},{\"name\":\"Sharon K. Parker\"}],\"year\":2021,\"source\":\"openalex\",\"journal\":\"Human Resource Management Review\",\"doi\":\"10.1016/j.hrmr.2021.100838\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hrmr.2021.100838\",\"sourceId\":\"https://openalex.org/W3161181201\",\"citationCount\":459,\"verificationState\":\"unverified\",\"evidenceId\":\"ev-2c95ac46-488b-4ba5-8eb6-c934700d730e\",\"projectId\":\"rp-248016a9-4b36-483c-be56-9054c9d4e9ac\"},{\"id\":\"lit-537b66f8-5b1f-494a-b118-102c8ad3d2f7\",\"title\":\"The duality of algorithmic management: Toward a research agenda on HRM algorithms, autonomy and value creation\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Jeroen Meijerink\"},{\"name\":\"Tanya Bondarouk\"}],\"year\":2021,\"source\":\"openalex\",\"journal\":\"Human Resource Management Review\",\"abstract\":\"This study proposes the 'duality of algorithmic management' as a conceptual lens to unravel the complex relationship between human resource management (HRM) algorithms, job autonomy and the value to workers who are subject to algorithmic management. Against tendencies to present algorithmic management as having predetermined, undesired consequences (e.g. restriction of job autonomy, poor financial compensation and deteriorating working conditions), our 'duality of algorithmic management' perspective offers two amendments to the dominant thinking on HRM algorithms and their outcomes to workers. First, we showcase how algorithmic management simultaneously restrains and enables autonomy and value to workers – with the latter referring to both use (i.e. non-monetary benefits) and exchange value (i.e. monetary benefits) that workers derive from working (under algorithmic management). In doing so, we make the case that the desired consequences of HRM algorithms to workers co-exist alongside the undesired consequences that the literature has mostly reported on. Second, we argue that algorithmic management is shaped by, as much as it shaping, the autonomy and value to workers. We do so by highlighting the 'recursivity' of algorithmic management that occurs when software designers and/or self-learning algorithms reinforce or limit worker acts for (re)gaining job autonomy and/or creating value out of HRM algorithms. We conclude this paper with the presentation of avenues for future research into the duality of algorithmic management, which sets the stage for a future line of inquiry into the complex interrelationships among HRM algorithms, job autonomy and value.\",\"doi\":\"10.1016/j.hrmr.2021.100876\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hrmr.2021.100876\",\"sourceId\":\"https://openalex.org/W3208464903\",\"citationCount\":288,\"verificationState\":\"unverified\",\"evidenceId\":\"ev-c4582e2b-759b-43db-8de9-07be45a00b99\",\"projectId\":\"rp-248016a9-4b36-483c-be56-9054c9d4e9ac\"}]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"c444a535-878a-406e-ba31-d80788bc77bb"}},"failed":false},{"name":"artifact_register","args":"{\"path\":\"review-draft.md\",\"title\":\"审读报告（草稿）\",\"type\":\"review\",\"note\":\"登记 workspace 中已有文件 review-draft.md 为 review 类型成果。当前内容为单行占位标题，尚无实质审读意见。\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":3,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_e081dd1718e041db893b4b2b"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_e081dd1718e041db893b4b2b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"ok\":true,\"artifact\":{\"id\":\"art-e6229689-d965-4d6b-974a-41e14f009765\",\"type\":\"review\",\"title\":\"审读报告（草稿）\",\"workspacePath\":\"review-draft.md\",\"projectId\":\"rp-248016a9-4b36-483c-be56-9054c9d4e9ac\",\"version\":1,\"source\":\"dsh-workspace\",\"status\":\"draft\",\"createdAt\":1789346320358,\"updatedAt\":1789346320358,\"evidenceIds\":[],\"versions\":[{\"artifactId\":\"art-e6229689-d965-4d6b-974a-41e14f009765\",\"version\":1,\"workspacePath\":\"review-draft.md\",\"contentHash\":\"11de0c75562e74dfd3381ca61f1eff3936657f25422f38aee5adbb94ed3a5898\",\"createdBy\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1789346320358,\"note\":\"登记 workspace 中已有文件 review-draft.md 为 review 类型成果。当前内容为单行占位标题，尚无实质审读意见。\"}]},\"error\":\"\"}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"aabeff62-4231-4cbf-9bdd-e1cd1d6ecfc8"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_get","args":"{\"doi\":\"10.1177/20539517211020332\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":3,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_4c6fa09871244006be9c6eda"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_4c6fa09871244006be9c6eda","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"record\":{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1177/20539517211020332\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic management in a work context\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Mohammad Hossein Jarrahi\"},{\"name\":\"Gemma Newlands\"},{\"name\":\"Min Kyung Lee\"},{\"name\":\"Christine T. Wolf\"},{\"name\":\"Eliscia Kinder\"},{\"name\":\"Will Sutherland\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Big Data &amp; Society\",\"abstract\":\"The rapid development of machine-learning algorithms, which underpin contemporary artificial intelligence systems, has created new opportunities for the automation of work processes and management functions. While algorithmic management has been observed primarily within the platform-mediated gig economy, its transformative reach and consequences are also spreading to more standard work settings. Exploring algorithmic management as a sociotechnical concept, which reflects both technological infrastructures and organizational choices, we discuss how algorithmic management may influence existing power and social structures within organizations. We identify three key issues. First, we explore how algorithmic management shapes pre-existing power dynamics between workers and managers. Second, we discuss how algorithmic management demands new roles and competencies while also fostering oppositional attitudes toward algorithms. Third, we explain how algorithmic management impacts knowledge and information exchange within an organization, unpacking the concept of opacity on both a technical and organizational level. We conclude by situating this piece in broader discussions on the future of work, accountability, and identifying future research steps.\",\"doi\":\"10.1177/20539517211020332\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1177/20539517211020332\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":435}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"579bf230-fbd3-4bc7-84d7-973c90f0a7ae"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_get","args":"{\"doi\":\"10.1016/j.hrmr.2021.100838\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":3,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_11d8584a3e9344d9bc40db1f"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_11d8584a3e9344d9bc40db1f","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"record\":{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1016/j.hrmr.2021.100838\",\"title\":\"Algorithms as work designers: How algorithmic manag